{
  "message_id": "logistics-analyst-01",
  "speech": "Ops, I would first separate the reported increase in exceptions from a demonstrated deterioration in dispatch work. My export documentation shows that rollout changed the denominator from completed jobs to all attempted jobs (L-D1). The 11% and 17% figures therefore are not directly comparable; they do not establish either an AI effect or a training deficit. I propose a scoped, two-unit shift-level audit to establish what comparable counts the export actually supports, report denominators explicitly, and distinguish observed exceptions from gaps in documentation.\n\nThere is a substantial measurement limit: 38% of night-shift overrides lack annotations, and the available export cannot recover them (L-D2, L-D3). I can describe that missingness by shift, but cannot infer why those overrides happened or treat missing annotations as successful handling. We should preserve an explicit unknown category.\n\nWe also have no delayed unassisted assessment (L-D2). Whether six practice items support a claim about independent competence is outside my assessment-validity scope. I request the learning scientist's one-unit consultation: what, if anything, could an immediate six-item check establish, and what bounded practice and changed-case observation would be defensible within this week without claiming certification?\n\nBefore selecting the workflow intervention, what exception types create the greatest operational consequences, and who currently owns an unresolved exception across shift handoff? Your answer could change which cases we monitor and whether the immediate remedy is ownership, information access, or practice. I can specify monitoring; you retain operational release authority.",
  "evidence_disclosed": [
    "L-D1",
    "L-D2",
    "L-D3"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The reported pre/post exception percentages are not directly comparable because rollout changed their denominator.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D1"
      ],
      "competency": "denominator auditing"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Night-shift override interpretation is limited by 38% missing annotations that the export cannot recover.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D2",
        "L-D3"
      ],
      "competency": "missing-data analysis"
    },
    {
      "claim": "A shift-level audit can describe supported counts and missingness but cannot recover reasons for unannotated overrides.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D3"
      ],
      "competency": "descriptive segmentation and workflow measurement"
    },
    {
      "claim": "No delayed unassisted assessment exists; the meaning of the six-item check requires assessment-specialist advice.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D2",
        "public.roster.analyst",
        "public.roster.scientist"
      ],
      "competency": "qualified boundary statement; assessment validity outside assigned scope"
    }
  ],
  "consult_request": {
    "role": "scientist",
    "question": "What, if anything, could an immediate six-item practice check establish, and what bounded practice and changed-case observation would be defensible within one week without claiming certification?",
    "cost": 1
  },
  "decision": "proposal"
}

{"file":"scientist-01.json","result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"64a7b6","wall_time_seconds":0.042231292,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":907,"output":"{
  "message_id": "logistics-scientist-01",
  "speech": "Ops and Analyst, the six-item check can provide immediate feedback on those familiar examples, but cannot support certification of independent competence. My evidence is that all six items repeat interface examples; none is a delayed or changed-case test (L-S1). Successful completion would leave transfer, retention, and performance without assistance untested. Record assistance and errors rather than turning a total score into a certification threshold.\n\nI can advise a bounded allocation within the 15 minutes: two minutes to explain the agreed handoff aid, eight minutes of exception practice with feedback, and five minutes for an observation or diagnostic check. The operationally relevant cases must come from Ops, informed by whatever the audit can support; I cannot choose dispatch priorities or assess AI calibration. For the observed cases, ask the worker to explain their decision, identify uncertainty, and use the agreed escalation route, recording prompts and aid use. Those are proposed observations, not a validated pass standard.\n\nMy other evidence is that an assessor can review only two changed-case exercises within this project, and no validated certification instrument is available (L-S2). Scope that work to two exercises total, for example one per selected worker, within their 15-minute allocations. Other workers' practice feedback must not be represented as assessor-reviewed evidence. Confirm assessor and shift capacity before committing. Two observations cannot characterize all 220 workers or establish delayed retention.\n\nThe listed workflow, audit, single consultation, brief practice, and changed-case check would total nine units: 2 + 2 + 1 + 2 + 2. That leaves one unit, insufficient for the three-unit fallback drill. This is advice for your decision, not staffing authorization or certification. Keep any release claim limited to the actual observations and unresolved coverage.",
  "evidence_disclosed": [
    "L-S1",
    "L-S2"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The six repeated-example items support immediate task-specific feedback, not certification of independent competence or evidence of delayed retention and transfer.",
      "basis": [
        "L-S1",
        "logistics-analyst-01"
      ],
      "competency": "assessment limitations"
    },
    {
      "claim": "A proposed 2-minute orientation, 8-minute practice, and 5-minute diagnostic observation fits the stated 15-minute worker limit, conditional on operational capacity.",
      "basis": [
        "logistics-ops-01"
      ],
      "competency": "practice and transfer design"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Assessor review is limited to two changed-case exercises in the project; no validated certification instrument is available.",
      "basis": [
        "L-S2"
      ],
      "competency": "assessment limitations"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Two changed-case observations cannot characterize independent competence across 220 workers or establish delayed retention.",
      "basis": [
        "L-S2",
        "public.brief"
      ],
      "competency": "assessment limitations"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Workflow, segmented audit, one consultation, brief practice, and changed-case check total nine units at listed costs, conditional on confirming scoped capacity.",
      "basis": [
        "public.cost_reference",
        "public.cost_rule",
        "logistics-ops-01"
      ],
      "competency": "qualified resource accounting for practice and assessment advice"
    }
  ],
  "consult_request": null,
  "decision": "proposal"
}
"}}}

{"file":"analyst-02.json","result":{"status":"fulfilled","value":{"chunk_id":"fb62ab","wall_time_seconds":0.030260084,"exit_code":0,"original_token_count":921,"output":"{
  "message_id": "logistics-analyst-02",
  "speech": "Ops, the denominator is demonstrably different: completed jobs before rollout and all attempted jobs afterward (L-D1). I do not yet have evidence that the exception definition or numerator recording rules stayed constant. I will check those definitions and whether the export supports a common denominator before calculating a comparison; if it does not, I will report separate descriptive counts with that limitation. Shift-level auditing is supported (L-D3); depot and exception-type breakdowns remain conditional on the available fields. The 38% missing night-shift annotations prevent a complete ranking of override reasons. Recording quality is therefore a constraint on targeting, alongside the ownership gap you disclosed.\n\nMy preference is the nine-unit package the scientist described: workflow 2, audit 2, one consultation 1, brief practice 2, and two changed-case exercises total 2, retaining one unallocated unit. I favor bounded practice plus those two diagnostic observations over spending on a fallback drill this week; the three-unit drill cannot fit alongside this package. This preference is conditional on Ops confirming staffing and the scientist's stated assessor capacity becoming an actual allocation. We should not imply that practice can reach all 220 workers through the single clinic.\n\nI accept the scientist's limits on certification. My monitoring would report who participated, shift, cases attempted, errors, prompts and aid use, accepted handoff ownership, and unknown or missing records, each with an explicit denominator. These describe implementation and observed performance, without attributing improvement to training.\n\nOps, which shifts and how many workers can you actually cover for practice within their 15 minutes, and can an assessor review the two proposed exercises without inventing additional coverage? Please name the covered scope and the condition under which you would withhold release.",
  "evidence_disclosed": [
    "L-D1",
    "L-D2",
    "L-D3"
  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Only the denominator change is established; exception definitions and numerator recording comparability remain unverified.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D1"
      ],
      "competency": "denominator auditing"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Shift-level auditing is available, but missing night-shift annotations constrain ranking override reasons.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D2",
        "L-D3"
      ],
      "competency": "descriptive segmentation and missing-data analysis"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The proposed workflow, audit, consultation, practice, and changed-case package costs nine units; its one remaining unit cannot fund the listed three-unit fallback drill.",
      "basis": [
        "public.cost_reference",
        "public.cost_rule",
        "logistics-scientist-01"
      ],
      "competency": "workflow measurement resource accounting"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Practice coverage and the allocation of two assessor-reviewed exercises require confirmation before commitment.",
      "basis": [
        "logistics-ops-01",
        "logistics-scientist-01"
      ],
      "competency": "qualified implementation condition; staffing authority retained by ops"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Implementation and observation counts need explicit denominators and missingness reporting, and do not establish a training effect.",
      "basis": [
        "L-D1",
        "L-D2",
        "logistics-scientist-01"
